1. Optimized Linux Storage Management

Storage Management:
*The server has a total storage capacity of 50 GB. 
*The storage can be divided into separate partitions for the operating system, system logs, user files, and application data. 
*A 10 GB partition is reserved for logs, and log rotation is enabled so that old logs are compressed or deleted automatically. 
*This prevents disk exhaustion and ensures critical services continue to operate.

Suggested allocation:

OS – 15 GB
System Logs – 10 GB
User Files – 15 GB
Application Data – 8 GB
Free/Reserve Space – 2 GB

CODE:

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    printf("Total Storage: 50 GB\n");
    printf("OS Partition: 15 GB\n");
    printf("Log Partition: 10 GB\n");
    printf("User Files: 15 GB\n");
    printf("Application Data: 8 GB\n");
    printf("Reserve Space: 2 GB\n");
    printf("Log Rotation: Enabled\n");
    printf("Critical Services: Protected\n");
    return 0;
}

OUTPUT:

Total Storage: 50 GB
OS Partition: 15 GB
Log Partition: 10 GB
User Files: 15 GB
Application Data: 8 GB
Reserve Space: 2 GB
Log Rotation: Enabled
Critical Services: Protected

2. CPU Workload Optimization

CPU Workload Optimization:

*The Linux system is experiencing delays due to frequent context switching under high CPU workload. 
*Since hardware upgrades are not possible, scheduler tuning, CPU affinity, process priority, and kernel modules can be used to improve performance. 
*CPU-intensive processes can be assigned appropriate CPU cores and priorities to reduce unnecessary context switching.

CODE:

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int cpu = 90;
    printf("CPU Usage: %d%%\n", cpu);
    printf("Frequent Context Switching Detected\n");
    printf("Scheduler Tuning: Enabled\n");
    printf("CPU Affinity: Applied\n");
    printf("Process Priority: Optimized\n");
    printf("Kernel Modules: Used\n");
    return 0;
}

