4. Faulty Process Management

Faulty Process Management:
*A process consuming 90% of CPU resources can affect other background and critical services. 
*The administrator can first identify the process using commands such as top or ps. 
*Its priority can then be reduced using renice. If the process continues to cause problems, it can be terminated using kill, without restarting the entire system.

Useful Linux commands:

top
ps aux
renice 10 -p PID
kill PID

CODE:

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int pid, cpu;
    printf("Enter Process ID: ");
    scanf("%d", &pid);
    printf("Enter CPU Usage: ");
    scanf("%d", &cpu);
    if (cpu >= 90) {
        printf("Faulty Process Detected\n");
        printf("PID: %d\n", pid);
        printf("Action: Reduce Priority or Terminate Process\n");
        printf("Critical Services: Protected\n");
    } else {
        printf("Process is operating normally\n");
    }
    return 0;
}

OUTPUT:

Enter Process ID: 2456
Enter CPU Usage: 90
Faulty Process Detected
PID: 2456
Action: Reduce Priority or Terminate Process
Critical Services: Protected

5. Virtual Machine RAM Allocation

VM RAM Allocation:
*The physical server has 16 GB RAM. 
*The host operating system requires 4 GB, leaving 12 GB for virtual machines. 
*Since each of the 5 VMs requires at least 2 GB, a total of 10 GB is allocated to the VMs. 
*The remaining 2 GB is kept as reserve memory for smooth operation and temporary workload requirements.

RAM allocation:

Host OS – 4 GB
VM1 – 2 GB
VM2 – 2 GB
VM3 – 2 GB
VM4 – 2 GB
VM5 – 2 GB
Reserve – 2 GB

CODE:

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int i;
    printf("Total RAM: 16 GB\n");
    printf("Host OS RAM: 4 GB\n");
    for(i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
        printf("VM%d RAM: 2 GB\n", i);
    printf("VM Allocated RAM: 10 GB\n");
    printf("Reserved RAM: 2 GB\n");
    printf("Resource Allocation: Completed\n");
    return 0;
}

OUTPUT:

Total RAM: 16 GB
Host OS RAM: 4 GB
VM1 RAM: 2 GB
VM2 RAM: 2 GB
VM3 RAM: 2 GB
VM4 RAM: 2 GB
VM5 RAM: 2 GB
VM Allocated RAM: 10 GB
